","ff66d4a449c00b40"],"groups":["4b46ba8e142021cf","5ac51bf06a3e0ac6","8e3d6a550157053d","2b2071572f551f27","8dfc5b9d3527a7cd","b8a31ff216a91db5","ff3f3f384b991065","5c2aab2cc6d30ef9","7c9050e74d184a1b","b5f44ad91e7a4e1c"],"findings":"**Review of the uncommitted impact/attention change (snapshot 201e7cc)**\n\nThe change is mostly lean. Each new piece has a caller, and the schema, format and carry logic each sit in one place. The suggestions below are small clarity and reuse cleanups. I did not apply any of them to the clone, so all are marked **read**. I did not run the test suite.\n\n1. **`src/commands/show.ts:222`, impact cell in the row format string.** The impact cell is built inline with a nested ternary and manual width arithmetic (`11 - r.g.group.impact.length - 2`). `necessityWord(n, pad)` in `src/render.ts:25` already does padding this way. Add `impactTag(i, pad)` on the same pattern, or a `impactCell(g, width)` helper, so the row becomes one readable expression. Size: small, about 6 lines. Benefit: clarity, and the padding stops depending on the bracket width. **read**\n\n2. **`src/commands/show.ts:42, 129-130`, repeated membership casts.** `(NECESSITY as readonly string[]).includes(n)` and the `IMPACT` equivalent appear three times, each with a cast. Export `isNecessity(x)` and `isImpact(x)` type guards from `src/schema.ts` next to the constants. `matchesOnly` and the unknown-value check then read as plain predicates. Size: small. **read**\n\n3. **`src/commands/show.ts` (about lines 157-161 and 191-192), two sort passes.** `groups` is sorted by impact before rendering, and then `sorted` is either `groups` or a necessity sort, depending on the same `values.sort === 'impact'` test. One comparator chosen once, which sorts by impact then necessity when `--sort impact` is set and by necessity otherwise, would let the `sorted` alias and the second branch go. Size: small. Benefit: one place decides order, and the reader doesn't have to match the two conditions. **read**\n\n4. **`src/carry.ts:72-73`, attention filtering.** The code spreads `a.attention`, overwrites `focus` with a possibly undefined value, and then deletes the key if it is empty. Compute the kept focus once, then build the object with a conditional spread: `const focus = a.attention?.focus?.filter((id) => keptIds.has(id))`, followed by `...(focus?.length ? { focus } : {})` on the rest of the object. This is the pattern `combineAttention` already uses, and it avoids mutating a freshly built object. Size: 3 lines. **read**\n\n5. **Lower priority: `src/merge.ts` (about lines 183-187), rename lookup.** The callback `(part, id) => newId.get(`${rated[part]}\\0${id}`)` makes the caller reach back into the index of `rated`, which is a second indirection to follow. Building one rename closure per rated source, or passing `(sourceIndex, id)` straight from the `used` mapping, would keep that detail out of `combineAttention`. This is a clarity point only, and I would skip it unless the merge code is already being touched. **read**\n\nDocumentation duplication (the impact vocabulary in README, SKILL.md, the schema descriptions and `IMPACT_HELP`) is inherent to having docs for agents and users, so I did not count it as a suggestion.\n\nVERDICT: ISSUES","verdict":"issues"}
